.TH symdump 1 "February 4, 2026"
.UC 2
.SH NAME
symdump \- dump symbol table of a program.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B symdump
[ executable ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B symdump
dumps the symbol table of a program to stdout one symbol per line in the form:
.sp
symbol_string type overlay value
.PP
The primary use of \fBsymdump\fP
is by \fBstrcompact\fP(1) when compacting the symbol table of the kernel but
nothing precludes more general use.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
need a file name: an executable's name is required.
.sp
cannot open: read permission to the file not granted
.sp
bad format: magic number of file is not valid for an executable
.sp
no name list: symbol table has been removed by \fBstrip\fP(1)
.sp
no string table: there is no string information for the name list to point to
.sp
no memory for strings: malloc failed to obtain memory to hold the string table
.sp
error reading strings: I/O error loading string table into memory
.PP
\fBsymdump\fP exits 0 if successful, and >0 if an error occurred.
.SH SEE ALSO
strcompact(1), symcompact(1), symorder(1)
.PP
/usr/include/nlist.h for symbol \fBtype\fP values.
